WEBVTT

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.330
Controllers make real-time decisions for industrial equipment.

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:05.250
A programmable logic controller, or PLC,

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.200
is commonly used for local machine and process control.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:13.010
It reads inputs, executes control logic, and updates outputs on a predictable cycle.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:17.445
PLCs are valued for reliability, deterministic behavior, and long service life.

00:00:17.445 --> 00:00:19.110
A remote terminal unit, or RTU,

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:23.160
supports telemetry and control at distributed or unattended locations.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.090
RTUs are common in utilities, pipelines, transportation, and water systems,

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.970
where communications may be constrained or intermittent.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.505
They can collect field data, execute local logic,

00:00:32.505 --> 00:00:34.850
and communicate with a central supervisory system.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:39.260
Distributed control systems use coordinated controllers across continuous or complex

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.000
processes.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.135
Their controllers, servers, operator stations,

00:00:42.135 --> 00:00:45.330
and engineering tools are usually designed as an integrated platform.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:49.230
Although product names differ, the controller still links process inputs, logic,

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.055
and outputs.

00:00:50.055 --> 00:00:54.495
Control logic may implement sequencing, interlocks, permissives, timing, calculations,

00:00:54.495 --> 00:00:56.060
alarms, or automatic regulation.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:00.644
Inputs represent measurements and states; outputs direct actuators or communicate results.

00:01:00.644 --> 00:01:03.500
A controller's operating mode, project, firmware,

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.730
and I/O configuration all contribute to trusted behavior.

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:08.130
Controller integrity is critical.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:11.145
An accidental change, corrupted project, incorrect firmware,

00:01:11.145 --> 00:01:14.840
or unauthorized action can alter the process or remove a protection.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:19.180
Availability also matters because loss of communication or control may force equipment

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:20.840
into a defined fallback state.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.865
Defensive controls include restricted engineering pathways, approved change procedures,

00:01:24.865 --> 00:01:29.040
protected project files, known-good backups, role-based access where supported,

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.990
and monitoring for unexpected programming or mode changes.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:35.620
Response must be coordinated with engineering: restarting, isolating,

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:40.400
or reloading a controller without process knowledge can create more risk than the original

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:40.410
event.
